  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section" id="intro">
  <div class="cs-chapter-grid">
    <div class="cs-chapter-heading"><h2>This time, the client was real, and so were the&nbsp;stakes.</h2></div>
    <div class="cs-chapter-content">
      <p class="cs-subtitle">Karlovy Vary is my hometown. Markéta, the founder, is a former classmate I found again years&nbsp;later.</p>
      <p>Working in a team of three, Julia, Paula and I set out to design the very first website for Galerie Venek, an independent outdoor contemporary art gallery that had been running for three years on Instagram alone. Unlike our first project, built around an imaginary client and a product that didn't exist, this one came with a real business, a real founder, and dozens of real exhibitions and workshops already behind it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section cs-section-surface" id="empathize">
  <div class="cs-chapter-grid">
    <div class="cs-chapter-heading"><h2>Even the people who found the gallery didn't believe what they&nbsp;found.</h2></div>
    <div class="cs-chapter-content">
      <p class="cs-subtitle">"It's like a first meeting," one person told us. Either the gallery shows who it is right away, or you fill in the blanks yourself.</p>
      <p>We spoke with Markéta first. Without a website, she told us, she felt like an amateur, even after three years of running the gallery with dozens of exhibitions and workshops behind her. Then we interviewed five people across Europe: a father in Karlovy Vary, a man in London who follows art but has little time for it, a woman in Paris who lives culture daily, a mother of two in London, and an artist in Oslo who applies to open calls across the continent. Six themes from our affinity map eventually merged into one: a website that doesn't feel alive and current makes a gallery invisible, even if it physically sits on a bridge a hundred people walk past every day.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section" id="define">
  <div class="cs-chapter-grid">
    <div class="cs-chapter-heading"><h2>Robin doesn't know if he can trust what he just&nbsp;found.</h2></div>
    <div class="cs-chapter-content">
      <p class="cs-subtitle">We named this the Confused Visitor stage: the lowest point in his whole&nbsp;journey.</p>

      <p>From the interviews, we shaped a persona: Robin, a 38-year-old father in Karlovy Vary. He's no art expert, but he doesn't shy away from cultural events if they're accessible and he can be sure his kids will have fun. He decides spontaneously, usually on Saturday mornings, and relies mainly on recommendations from friends because local listings rarely reach him.</p>

      <figure class="cs-visual-full cs-artifact cs-lightbox-figure">
        <img src="/assets/venek/persona-robin.jpg" alt="Persona card for Robin, 38, father in Karlovy Vary: his needs, frustrations, and how he decides where to go.">
      </figure>

      <p>We mapped his journey into seven steps, from spotting the gallery on Instagram to the decision to actually go. The turning point we named the Confused Visitor stage: Robin has found the gallery, but he has no idea whether the information is current or if it's even open this weekend.</p>

      <figure class="cs-visual-full cs-artifact cs-artifact-captioned cs-lightbox-figure" data-artifact-label="Journey map">
        <img src="/assets/venek/journey-map.jpg" alt="Robin's seven-step journey map, from spotting the gallery on Instagram to the Confused Visitor stage to the decision to go.">
      </figure>

      <p>That gap became our problem statement: parents with young children need an easy way to find and evaluate whether a gallery event is right for their family, because scattered and outdated information makes it hard to plan with confidence.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section cs-section-surface" id="ideate">
  <div class="cs-chapter-grid">
    <div class="cs-chapter-heading"><h2>Three questions decided the whole&nbsp;structure.</h2></div>
    <div class="cs-chapter-content">
      <p class="cs-subtitle">Real photos over generic templates. One card, not a click-through. Standalone pages, not one buried Instagram feed.</p>

      <p>From the problem statement, we drew three How Might We questions, each pointing at a concrete answer. Help parents trust a gallery they've never visited: real photos of the space and the people behind it, no generic template look. Let parents judge in seconds whether an event suits their family: a What's on section right below the hero, the nearest event as a single card with what, where, when, free or not, one line of description, no extra clicking. Make the site easy to find and share: a hybrid structure, a long-scroll homepage for the quick overview and standalone pages for the deeper detail, with What's on, About us, Exhibitions, Workshops, Getting here, Open call and Shop in the navigation.</p>

      <p>We generated dozens of ideas on a shared board, rated them together, then sorted them with MoSCoW: what the first version couldn't do without, what could wait, and what we cut entirely. Our MVP statement went through the same edit: a website where a parent can tell, within seconds, what's happening, when, and whether it's right for their family.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="cs-zone-z3 cs-spotlight">
<section class="cs-section" id="prototype">
  <div class="cs-chapter-grid">
    <div class="cs-chapter-heading"><h2>Booking had to feel like the easiest part, not the last hurdle.</h2></div>
    <div class="cs-chapter-content">
      <p class="cs-subtitle">This is where all the trust we'd built could still fall apart.</p>

      <p>While designing the event detail pages, we found the single biggest risk in the whole flow: the enrollment process itself. If booking is confusing, redirects to some outside platform, or doesn't make clear whether it's even necessary, everything we'd built to earn trust falls apart right at the moment it matters most.</p>

      <div class="cs-media-row">
        <div>
          <p>For the main flow, we followed Robin's own decision-making exactly. From the What's on section, he goes straight into a clean booking form: number of adults and children, an email, and a confirm button. Removing friction was the only goal, closing the gap between "this looks great" and "we have our spots booked."</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-media-visual cs-artifact cs-lightbox-figure">
          <img src="/assets/venek/workshop-booking.jpg" alt="Workshop detail page for Watercolours with Anna, with a booking widget for number of adults and children, email, and a confirm button.">
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-media-row reverse">
        <div>
          <p>At the end, he sees a clear success screen with the date, location, and a map, so he never has to click away to plan the actual visit.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-media-visual cs-artifact cs-lightbox-figure">
          <img src="/assets/venek/success-confirmation.jpg" alt="Booking success screen with confirmation details and a map showing the gallery's location.">
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>We designed the exhibition and workshop pages the same way, with real photos and a short profile of the artist, so Robin can judge an event's quality and atmosphere without checking Google first. We also tested the homepage's responsive behaviour on <a href="https://www.figma.com/proto/d6q6bX4VkVP77a8jUltF5G/P2-G5-E-Commerce-%7C-Tereza--Zol--Paula?node-id=13628-2943&viewport=-914%2C-410%2C0.08&t=GeGTopNWFPHGiSKr-1&scaling=scale-down&content-scaling=fixed&starting-point-node-id=13628%3A2943&show-proto-sidebar=1&page-id=13587%3A5809" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tablet</a> and <a href="https://www.figma.com/proto/d6q6bX4VkVP77a8jUltF5G/P2-G5-E-Commerce-%7C-Tereza--Zol--Paula?node-id=13628-2945&viewport=-10398%2C-4856%2C0.53&t=od7cevmG1BFC8EUg-1&scaling=scale-down&content-scaling=fixed&starting-point-node-id=13628%3A2945&show-proto-sidebar=1&page-id=13587%3A5809" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mobile</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<section class="cs-section cs-section-surface" id="test">
  <div class="cs-chapter-grid">
    <div class="cs-chapter-heading"><h2>Creativity is great, but usability always comes&nbsp;first.</h2></div>
    <div class="cs-chapter-content">
      <p class="cs-subtitle">The word "Enroll" made perfect sense to us. It confused everyone who wasn't&nbsp;us.</p>

      <p>We tested in two rounds using the think-aloud method, both times asking people to find an upcoming exhibition and book onto it. The clearest finding came from a single button. We'd labelled it "Enroll now" on every event card, but for exhibitions that wording read like signing up for a class, not booking a one-off visit. Testers expected "Book now" or "Buy tickets" instead. We renamed the card button to "Book now" and kept "Enroll" only for the final step inside the flow, where someone actually enters their name and email.</p>

      <p>The same round also caught two smaller things: the "View all workshops" and "Exhibitions" links had been swapped, and transport information that testers valued was placed too late, after the decision was already made rather than during it. All three fixes point the same direction: you have to design for what people already expect, not for the words that make sense inside your own head.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section" id="reflection">
  <div class="cs-chapter-grid">
    <div class="cs-chapter-heading"><h2>The UX process is a living organism, not a&nbsp;checklist.</h2></div>
    <div class="cs-chapter-content">
      <p class="cs-subtitle">As an artist, I had to learn where the wow stops and usability has to win.</p>

      <p>My part was laying the groundwork across nearly every phase, which Julia and Paula then built further detail on top of. I brought the team up to speed on the local context of Karlovy Vary, analyzed the local competition, and navigated the Czech-specific details. In mid-fi, I owned the entire workshop enrollment flow and the whole What's on page, the part that decides whether someone like Robin can form a clear picture of an event within a few seconds. At the final presentation, I introduced the whole project myself, from the gallery and Markéta through Robin's journey to the problem statement.</p>

      <p>The biggest lesson was realizing the UX process is a living organism, not a linear list of tasks you check off once. We went back a phase more than once and reworked things that had stopped working in practice, and I carry that into my own work now: test, iterate, and don't force what isn't working. The other one is more personal: as an artist, I have to set myself clear boundaries in UX. A website shouldn't just be a visually dazzling piece; above all, it needs to feel familiar, calm, and intuitive.</p>

      <p>The result is the gallery's first website ever, built on the transparency and information that were missing before. Our shared team design work ends here, but the project doesn't: building and launching the real site is now entirely on me. You can read the full <a href="https://www.figma.com/proto/d6q6bX4VkVP77a8jUltF5G/P2-G5-E-Commerce-%7C-Tereza--Zol--Paula?node-id=13700-434&viewport=6368%2C-1300%2C0.13&t=QjIOgRRlRmgjLAMI-1&scaling=min-zoom&content-scaling=fixed&page-id=2%3A4135&desktop-link-click-timestamp=1789639955560&desktop-ul-exp-bucket=po" target="_blank" rel="noopener">presentation</a> or click through the <a href="https://www.figma.com/proto/d6q6bX4VkVP77a8jUltF5G/P2-G5-E-Commerce-%7C-Tereza--Zol--Paula?node-id=12974-3444&p=f&viewport=-1540%2C-909%2C0.13&t=Hn3UgBd5fhqmeT0k-1&scaling=scale-down&content-scaling=fixed&starting-point-node-id=12846%3A7670&show-proto-sidebar=1&page-id=801%3A8072" target="_blank" rel="noopener">prototype</a> yourself.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
